WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.222
Now the inside cases.

00:00:02.222 --> 00:00:04.419
Here is the tree 7

00:00:04.419 --> 00:00:07.257
with children 4 and 12

00:00:07.257 --> 00:00:09.363
after inserting 8.

00:00:09.363 --> 00:00:12.017
7 has balance -2.

00:00:12.017 --> 00:00:16.137
We went right at 7, then left at 12

00:00:16.137 --> 00:00:18.700
right-left, the bent shape.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.571
Try a single left rotation at 7 anyway.

00:00:22.571 --> 00:00:23.962
12 comes up

00:00:23.962 --> 00:00:26.337
7 goes down to the left.

00:00:26.337 --> 00:00:28.793
But look: the middle subtree

00:00:28.793 --> 00:00:31.167
11 with its child 8

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:33.623
simply moved across to 7.

00:00:33.705 --> 00:00:36.898
Now 12 has a left subtree of height

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.436
2 against the leaf 13

00:00:39.436 --> 00:00:40.910
balance +2.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.530
The program agrees: AVL, false.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.320
The single rotation just moves the

00:00:46.391 --> 00:00:48.257
problem to the other side

00:00:48.328 --> 00:00:50.983
because the middle subtree is exactly

00:00:51.055 --> 00:00:52.921
the part that is too deep.

00:00:52.921 --> 00:00:55.360
We need to look one level deeper.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.403
Take the mirror image, left-right

00:00:59.403 --> 00:01:01.033
with triangles.

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:03.580
The new key went into B

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:06.432
the middle subtree under y.

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:08.061
B is not empty

00:01:08.061 --> 00:01:11.219
it holds at least the new key

00:01:11.321 --> 00:01:13.969
so call its root x and its

00:01:14.071 --> 00:01:16.618
subtrees B1 and B2.

00:01:16.618 --> 00:01:20.896
The keys are in the order A, y, B1, x

00:01:20.896 --> 00:01:22.220
B2, z, C.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:24.962
The fix makes x the root

00:01:24.962 --> 00:01:29.171
with y and z as its children, in two steps.

00:01:29.171 --> 00:01:33.475
First, a left rotation at y lifts x above y.

00:01:33.475 --> 00:01:37.780
That turns the dog-leg into a straight line

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.650
z, x, y, the left-left shape.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.209
Second, a right rotation at z

00:01:44.209 --> 00:01:48.594
exactly as in part two, lifts x above z.

00:01:48.594 --> 00:01:52.337
x, the root of the middle subtree

00:01:52.337 --> 00:01:54.049
ends up on top.

00:01:54.049 --> 00:01:58.220
And the keys are still in order: A, y

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.680
B1, x, B2, z, C.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:04.491
The heights: if A and C have height h

00:02:04.577 --> 00:02:07.383
then B1 and B2 have heights

00:02:07.468 --> 00:02:10.274
h and h - 1, in some order.

00:02:10.274 --> 00:02:12.570
After the double rotation

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.717
y and z both have height h + 1

00:02:15.717 --> 00:02:18.183
and x has height h + 2

00:02:18.268 --> 00:02:20.649
again the height the subtree

00:02:20.734 --> 00:02:22.860
had before the insertion.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.195
In Weiss's code the double rotation is

00:02:26.267 --> 00:02:28.805
literally the two single rotations.

00:02:28.878 --> 00:02:31.342
doubleWithLeftChild handles the

00:02:31.415 --> 00:02:33.372
left-right case at k3.

00:02:33.445 --> 00:02:35.692
First it rotates k3's left

00:02:35.765 --> 00:02:37.722
child with its right child

00:02:37.795 --> 00:02:39.680
and stores the result back

00:02:39.752 --> 00:02:41.202
in k3.left.

00:02:41.275 --> 00:02:43.305
Then it rotates k3 with

00:02:43.377 --> 00:02:44.755
its new left child

00:02:44.755 --> 00:02:46.640
and returns the new root.

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:49.648
The right-left case is the mirror

00:02:49.648 --> 00:02:51.602
doubleWithRightChild.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.791
So the four cases need only

00:02:53.869 --> 00:02:57.230
two real methods: the two single rotations.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.273
Back to 7, 4, 12

00:03:00.273 --> 00:03:02.824
with 11 and 8 below.

00:03:02.824 --> 00:03:06.079
7 is z, 12 is y, and 11

00:03:06.079 --> 00:03:09.422
the root of the middle subtree, is x.

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:12.412
First a right rotation at 12

00:03:12.412 --> 00:03:13.908
11 comes up

00:03:13.908 --> 00:03:16.987
and 12 goes down to its right

00:03:16.987 --> 00:03:18.570
keeping 13.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.405
Then a left rotation at 7

00:03:21.405 --> 00:03:23.311
11 comes up again

00:03:23.311 --> 00:03:26.045
and 7 goes down to its left

00:03:26.045 --> 00:03:27.205
taking 8.

00:03:27.205 --> 00:03:30.519
11, with children 7 and 12.

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:34.081
Every balance factor is 0 or -1

00:03:34.081 --> 00:03:34.992
AVL, true.

00:03:34.992 --> 00:03:37.726
The middle node went to the top

00:03:37.726 --> 00:03:41.040
as it always does in a double rotation.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:42.521
Your turn.

00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:46.484
Here is the tree from part two: 3, 2

00:03:46.484 --> 00:03:50.177
7, 5, 9, after inserting 4.

00:03:50.177 --> 00:03:53.059
The root has balance -2.

00:03:53.059 --> 00:03:56.032
Which of the four cases is this?

00:03:56.122 --> 00:03:58.554
Which node becomes the root

00:03:58.644 --> 00:04:00.806
of the repaired subtree

00:04:00.806 --> 00:04:03.328
and what is the final tree?

00:04:03.328 --> 00:04:06.300
Pause the video and work it out.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:12.451
The deepest unbalanced node

00:04:12.546 --> 00:04:14.261
is the root 3.

00:04:14.261 --> 00:04:17.498
We went right at 3, to 7

00:04:17.498 --> 00:04:21.403
then left at 7, to 5: right-left

00:04:21.403 --> 00:04:25.021
an inside case, so a double rotation.

00:04:25.021 --> 00:04:28.640
A right rotation at 7 lifts 5.

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:31.296
Then a left rotation at 3

00:04:31.375 --> 00:04:32.727
lifts 5 again.

00:04:32.727 --> 00:04:34.158
The result: 5

00:04:34.158 --> 00:04:36.622
with children 3 and 7

00:04:36.622 --> 00:04:38.530
3 has 2 and 4

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:41.154
and 7 has 9 on the right.

00:04:41.154 --> 00:04:43.777
The program prints exactly this.

00:04:43.777 --> 00:04:46.799
5, the root of the middle subtree

00:04:46.799 --> 00:04:48.150
ended up on top.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:50.957
A common mistake is rotating

00:04:51.036 --> 00:04:52.467
at the wrong node.

00:04:52.547 --> 00:04:54.932
The rotation is centred on the

00:04:55.012 --> 00:04:57.159
deepest unbalanced node, z

00:04:57.159 --> 00:04:59.942
not on the root of the whole tree

00:04:59.942 --> 00:05:01.930
and not on the new leaf.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.564
And read the names on figures carefully.

00:05:05.646 --> 00:05:08.684
A right rotation at 12 means that

00:05:08.766 --> 00:05:11.230
12 goes down to the right

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:13.611
and its left child comes up.

00:05:13.693 --> 00:05:15.910
The rotation is named after

00:05:15.992 --> 00:05:17.880
the node it happens at.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.404
Now the whole insertion algorithm.

00:05:21.404 --> 00:05:25.213
To insert x into the tree t: if t is empty

00:05:25.213 --> 00:05:26.858
return a new leaf.

00:05:26.858 --> 00:05:29.455
If x is smaller than t's key

00:05:29.455 --> 00:05:32.313
insert it into the left subtree.

00:05:32.399 --> 00:05:35.256
If that subtree is now two taller

00:05:35.343 --> 00:05:37.940
than the right one, rebalance.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.286
If x is smaller than the

00:05:40.369 --> 00:05:42.199
key of t's left child

00:05:42.199 --> 00:05:45.027
x went to the outside: left-left

00:05:45.027 --> 00:05:46.607
a single rotation.

00:05:46.607 --> 00:05:50.350
Otherwise it went to the inside: left-right

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:51.930
a double rotation.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:53.555
If x is larger

00:05:53.555 --> 00:05:56.444
do the mirror image on the right

00:05:56.444 --> 00:05:58.823
if it is equal, do nothing.

00:05:58.823 --> 00:06:02.477
Finally recompute t's height and return t

00:06:02.562 --> 00:06:05.111
or the new root of the subtree

00:06:05.196 --> 00:06:06.640
after a rotation.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:09.720
Here is the code from the slides

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.311
which follows Weiss.

00:06:11.387 --> 00:06:13.887
The recursive call stores the new

00:06:13.963 --> 00:06:16.008
left subtree in t.left

00:06:16.084 --> 00:06:18.205
and that subtree has already

00:06:18.281 --> 00:06:20.250
recomputed its own height.

00:06:20.326 --> 00:06:22.372
Then t compares the heights

00:06:22.447 --> 00:06:23.963
of its two subtrees.

00:06:23.963 --> 00:06:27.220
A difference of two means t is unbalanced.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:31.287
Why is comparing x with the key of t's left

00:06:31.373 --> 00:06:34.571
child enough to tell the cases apart?

00:06:34.571 --> 00:06:38.201
Because t is the deepest unbalanced node

00:06:38.288 --> 00:06:40.881
and x is in the taller subtree

00:06:40.967 --> 00:06:42.523
of t's left child.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.684
Which side of that child

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:46.672
x went to is the case.

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:49.524
Smaller: rotateWithLeftChild.

00:06:49.524 --> 00:06:52.290
Larger: doubleWithLeftChild.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:54.426
And because the test happens

00:06:54.489 --> 00:06:57.104
inside the recursion, on the way back up

00:06:57.168 --> 00:06:59.463
the first unbalanced node we meet is

00:06:59.527 --> 00:07:01.440
automatically the deepest one.

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:05.069
Now trace insertions with heights

00:07:05.069 --> 00:07:08.566
on the tree 20, 10, 30, 5

00:07:08.566 --> 00:07:10.897
25, 35.

00:07:10.897 --> 00:07:12.152
Insert 40.

00:07:12.152 --> 00:07:16.008
The path goes 20, 30, 35

00:07:16.098 --> 00:07:18.519
and 40 becomes the right

00:07:18.608 --> 00:07:20.491
child of 35.

00:07:20.491 --> 00:07:24.347
Going back up: 35 gets height 1

00:07:24.437 --> 00:07:26.409
and balance −1.

00:07:26.409 --> 00:07:30.265
30 gets height 2, balance −1.

00:07:30.265 --> 00:07:34.300
20 gets height 3, balance −1.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:36.900
All within 1: no rotation.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:38.884
Insert 45

00:07:38.884 --> 00:07:41.770
it becomes the right child of 40.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:45.280
Going back up, 40 gets balance −1

00:07:45.358 --> 00:07:48.556
and 35 has an empty left subtree

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:51.286
and a right subtree of height 1

00:07:51.286 --> 00:07:52.768
balance −2.

00:07:52.768 --> 00:07:56.200
35 is the deepest unbalanced node.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.989
We went right at 35

00:07:59.076 --> 00:08:01.864
and right at 40: right-right

00:08:01.864 --> 00:08:05.261
a single left rotation at 35.

00:08:05.261 --> 00:08:08.658
40 is now the root of that subtree

00:08:08.658 --> 00:08:12.316
with children 35 and 45

00:08:12.316 --> 00:08:13.361
height 1

00:08:13.361 --> 00:08:17.281
the height 35's subtree had before.

00:08:17.281 --> 00:08:20.678
Above it, 30 and 20 keep their

00:08:20.765 --> 00:08:23.030
heights and their balance.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:27.150
Insert 34: the path goes 20

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:29.568
30, 40, 35

00:08:29.654 --> 00:08:31.986
and 34 becomes the

00:08:32.072 --> 00:08:34.317
left child of 35.

00:08:34.317 --> 00:08:38.030
Going back up, 35 has height 1

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:41.743
40 has height 2 and balance +1

00:08:41.829 --> 00:08:44.851
and 30 has the leaf 25

00:08:44.937 --> 00:08:48.650
against a subtree of height 2: −2.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.375
Right at 30, then left at 40

00:08:52.375 --> 00:08:55.281
right-left, a double rotation.

00:08:55.281 --> 00:08:59.499
A right rotation at 40 lifts 35.

00:08:59.593 --> 00:09:01.937
A left rotation at 30

00:09:02.030 --> 00:09:04.280
lifts 35 again.

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:07.037
35 is the subtree root

00:09:07.037 --> 00:09:10.345
with children 30 and 40, height 2

00:09:10.345 --> 00:09:13.278
again the height before the insertion.

00:09:13.278 --> 00:09:15.910
And 20 keeps its height 3.

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:18.717
Look at the four cases again.

00:09:18.717 --> 00:09:19.792
In every one

00:09:19.868 --> 00:09:23.016
the rotated subtree ends up with the same

00:09:23.093 --> 00:09:25.780
height it had before the insertion.

00:09:25.857 --> 00:09:28.084
So every node above it sees a

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:30.310
subtree of unchanged height

00:09:30.387 --> 00:09:32.690
and its height and balance are

00:09:32.767 --> 00:09:34.456
what they were before

00:09:34.456 --> 00:09:37.220
when the tree was a valid AVL tree.

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:38.756
After an insertion

00:09:38.756 --> 00:09:41.443
at most one rebalancing is needed

00:09:41.443 --> 00:09:44.130
one single or one double rotation.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:47.934
The cost: the search down is O(log n).

00:09:47.934 --> 00:09:49.619
On the way back up

00:09:49.619 --> 00:09:52.315
each node compares two heights

00:09:52.315 --> 00:09:55.854
maybe rotates, and recomputes its height

00:09:55.854 --> 00:09:57.708
all in constant time.

00:09:57.792 --> 00:10:00.151
So insertion costs O(log n)

00:10:00.235 --> 00:10:01.920
in the worst case.

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:05.509
Sorted keys were the worst case for

00:10:05.595 --> 00:10:07.916
a plain binary search tree.

00:10:07.916 --> 00:10:09.980
Insert 1, 2, 3

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:12.559
up to 7 into an AVL tree.

00:10:12.559 --> 00:10:14.536
2 goes right of 1.

00:10:14.536 --> 00:10:18.061
3 makes 1 unbalanced: right-right

00:10:18.061 --> 00:10:21.500
one left rotation, and 2 is the root.

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:24.113
4 goes to the right.

00:10:24.113 --> 00:10:27.163
5 unbalances 3: rotate.

00:10:27.163 --> 00:10:30.803
6 unbalances the root 2: rotate

00:10:30.803 --> 00:10:33.360
and 4 is the new root.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:36.410
7 unbalances 5: rotate.

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:39.895
The result is the perfect tree 4, 2

00:10:39.895 --> 00:10:41.959
6, after 4 rotations

00:10:41.959 --> 00:10:44.176
each a single left rotation.

00:10:44.252 --> 00:10:46.928
With 1023 sorted

00:10:47.004 --> 00:10:49.603
keys the AVL tree has height 9

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:51.973
a plain tree would have height

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:53.884
1022.

00:10:53.884 --> 00:10:56.560
Sorted input is no problem at all.

00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:57.976
To sum up.

00:10:57.976 --> 00:11:01.654
One: in the inside cases a double rotation

00:11:01.654 --> 00:11:04.245
which is two single rotations

00:11:04.245 --> 00:11:07.087
lifts the middle node to the top.

00:11:07.087 --> 00:11:10.431
Two: insert as in a binary search tree

00:11:10.431 --> 00:11:12.520
then on the way back up

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:15.780
rotate at the deepest unbalanced node.

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:19.618
Three: one rebalancing gives the subtree

00:11:19.706 --> 00:11:22.932
back its old height, so it is enough

00:11:22.932 --> 00:11:25.810
and insertion costs O(log n).

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:27.098
Try this.

00:11:27.098 --> 00:11:31.790
Insert 50, 25, 10, 5, 7

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:35.855
3, 30, 20, 8, 15

00:11:35.855 --> 00:11:36.585
60

00:11:36.585 --> 00:11:41.068
70 and 80 into an empty AVL tree.

00:11:41.172 --> 00:11:44.716
Draw the tree after each insertion

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:47.322
and name every rotation.

00:11:47.322 --> 00:11:49.720
All four cases appear.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.054
In part four: deletion

00:11:52.054 --> 00:11:55.590
where one rotation is not always enough.

